# Apply Notes — registry entry and final deploy steps

The registry update **triggers the preview upload**, so it is the last thing you do. Run the checks in
order; nothing here could be executed from the authoring session (no shell, and `projects/projects.json`
lives outside that sandbox).

---

## Order matters — do not skip ahead

| # | Step | Why it comes here |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Restore the 19 missing source files into the site root | Nothing renders without them |
| 2 | Run the four local verification commands (README §Local verification) | Prove the build before it ships |
| 3 | **Delete `qa-smoke.html`** | It seeds browser profiles and must never ship |
| 4 | Confirm `README.md`, `docs/`, `deploy/` are excluded from the bundle | A static root would serve them publicly |
| 5 | Append the registry entry (below) | This is what triggers the preview upload |

---

## Step 3 — remove the QA page

```powershell
cd <siteRoot>
Remove-Item .\qa-smoke.html -Force -ErrorAction Stop
Test-Path .\qa-smoke.html        # expect: False
```

Also remove any leftover QA scaffolding that is not part of the app: `qa-smoke.js`, `qa-ui.js`,
`patch-qa.py`, `extract_pdfs2.py`, `extract_assets.py`. None of these are app code.

---

## Step 5 — append the registry entry

The object to append is in `deploy/projects.entry.json`. Target file: `projects/projects.json`, which
is a **top-level JSON array** — append to it, never replace it. Existing items and any
`deploymentResult` fields must survive untouched.

```powershell
# --- from the workspace root that contains projects\ ---

# 1. Back up first. If the append goes wrong you want the original back.
Copy-Item 'projects\projects.json' 'projects\projects.json.bak' -Force

# 2. Load both files
$registryPath = 'projects\projects.json'
$entryPath    = 'deploy\projects.entry.json'
$registry = Get-Content $registryPath -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
$entry    = Get-Content $entryPath    -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json

# 3. Refuse to create a duplicate
if (@($registry) | Where-Object { $_.id -eq $entry.id }) {
  throw "Entry '$($entry.id)' already present - aborting so nothing is duplicated."
}

# 4. Preserve a real timestamp
$now = (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().ToString('yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ')
$entry.createdAt = $now
$entry.updatedAt = $now

# 5. Append and write
$updated = @($registry) + @($entry)
$updated | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 20 | Set-Content $registryPath -Encoding utf8
```

### Validate before trusting it

```powershell
$v = Get-Content 'projects\projects.json' -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json

# a) parses as a top-level array
if ($v -isnot [System.Array]) { throw 'Not an array' }

# b) expected total count (original items + 1)
$v.Count

# c) target id present exactly once
@($v | Where-Object { $_.id -eq 'website-8fb4f824e29ad0478ac0cbed' }).Count   # expect: 1

# d) no other project lost
Compare-Object `
  (@(Get-Content 'projects\projects.json.bak' -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json).id) `
  $v.id | Where-Object SideIndicator -eq '<='      # expect: nothing listed
```

If (a)–(d) all pass, the preview upload fires. If anything fails, restore and retry:

```powershell
Copy-Item 'projects\projects.json.bak' 'projects\projects.json' -Force
```

### Two caveats

- `ConvertTo-Json` reformats whitespace and escapes non-ASCII characters. That is harmless for a JSON
  parser, but if `projects.json` is under version control you will see a large diff. Read the diff or
  use a JSON-aware editor if you would rather keep formatting stable.
- `ConvertTo-Json` in Windows PowerShell 5.1 is depth-limited; `-Depth 20` is deliberate. Do not lower
  it, or nested `deployment` fields will be truncated to strings.

