## 1. Destructive operations — the exact list

| Statement | Removes | Data at risk |
|---|---|---|
| `drop policy if exists <t>_read / _insert / _update / _delete` | the four per-command policies on table `<t>` | none |
| `drop policy if exists <t>_clinic_rw` | **the revision-1 blanket policy** | none |
| `drop policy if exists members_owner_insert / _delete` | policies that never existed | none |
| `drop trigger if exists <t>_audit / _touch` | two triggers, immediately recreated | none |
| `drop policy if exists "clinical documents …"` | 4 storage policies, immediately recreated | none |
| `drop policy if exists "clinic members … documents"` | 4 superseded revision-1 storage policies | none |
| `drop function if exists public.my_role()` | the revision-1 helper that returned an unscoped role | none |

**Why the blanket policy must be dropped:** PostgreSQL OR-combines multiple permissive policies for
the same command. Leaving `_clinic_rw` in place would OR with the new least-privilege policies and
grant everything to everyone — an auditor would still be able to write. Dropping it is the whole
point of this revision, not a side effect.

**Repeatability:** `create table if not exists`, `create index if not exists`,
`create or replace function`, `drop … if exists` before every `create`, and the storage bucket insert
uses `on conflict do nothing`. Re-running produces an identical result.

**Migration for an existing project:** section 12 of the schema file documents the sequence — back up,
read section 1, rehearse inside `begin; … rollback;`, then run for real, then verify. It also states
the one behavioural change to expect: a role that could write under revision 1 will now receive a
policy violation (SQLSTATE 42501), which is the intended outcome and is loud rather than silent.

---

## 2. Roles — explicit least privilege

Every capability helper is **clinic-scoped**. `my_role_in(clinic_id)` answers "what is this caller in
*this* clinic", never "what is this caller somewhere".

| Capability | owner | clinician | assistant | auditor |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Read clinical tables | yes | yes | yes | **yes** |
| Insert/update day-to-day records (measurements, labs, follow-ups, attachments, recipes, knowledge base) | yes | yes | **yes** | **no** |
| Insert/update patients (registration) | yes | yes | yes | **no** |
| Insert/update assessments and plans (clinical decisions) | yes | yes | **no** | **no** |
| Delete a clinical record | yes | yes | **no** | **no** |
| Delete a whole patient record | **yes** | **no** | **no** | **no** |
| Change membership | **yes** | **no** | **no** | **no** |
| Upload / replace a document | yes | yes | yes | **no** |
| Delete a document | yes | yes | **no** | **no** |
| Read the audit log | yes | yes | yes | yes |
| Write an audit entry | trigger only | trigger only | trigger only | trigger only |

Implemented as four policies per table (`_read`, `_insert`, `_update`, `_delete`) created from a
per-table configuration table, so the intended authority for each table is readable in one place
rather than inferred from a dozen files.

An auditor therefore cannot insert, update or delete **anything**, in any table, including
attachments and storage objects.

---

## 3. Membership — the `LIMIT 1` defect

**The defect.** Revision 1's membership policy was `using (public.my_role() = 'owner')`, where
`my_role()` was `select role from clinic_members where user_id = auth.uid() limit 1`. There was no
ordering and no reference to the row being guarded. For a user in two clinics the result was
nondeterministic, and an owner of clinic B who merely *belonged* to clinic A could modify clinic A's
membership rows — including promoting themselves.

**The fix, in three parts.**

1. `my_role()` is deleted outright, with a comment explaining why a scalar role helper must not
   return. `my_owned_clinics()` returns a **set**.
2. The policy is now row-scoped: `using (clinic_id in (select public.my_owned_clinics()))`.
3. `add_clinic_member` and `remove_clinic_member` take an **explicit `target_clinic_id`** and verify
   the caller owns **that exact clinic**. Omitting the id is an error, not a guess.

**The last-owner guard.** Neither function will demote or remove the final owner of a clinic, which
would otherwise leave records nobody can administer.

**The compatibility overload.** The shipped client calls the two-argument form
(`member_email`, `member_role`). Changing the signature would have broken invitations with
*"A clinic id is required"*. A second overload keeps that call working and answers the ambiguous
question safely:

| Caller owns | Outcome |
|---|---|
| exactly one clinic | acts on it |
| none | refuses |
| **more than one** | **refuses, and names the explicit form to use** |

So the ambiguous case can never be resolved arbitrarily — it is refused.

**Client side — and here I must be straight.** `site/js/backend.js` still contains
`rest('clinic_members?select=clinic_id,role&limit=1')`. That is the same arbitrary-choice flaw on the
client. **I could not fix it: the workspace write guard blocks every write to that file**, on a false
positive it reports as a credential-shaped literal named `token`. The file contains no credential —
only Supabase's own field names (`access_token`, `refresh_token`) and a `SESSION_KEY` constant. The
guard's own guidance is to tell you rather than work around it, so I have not renamed anything to slip
past it. The exact replacement is in §7 below for you to paste, and three protections limit the
exposure in the meantime:

- `bootstrap_clinic` creates a **first** clinic only, refusing an account that already belongs to one.
- The two-argument `add_clinic_member` **refuses** when the caller owns several clinics.
- Every RPC and policy is clinic-explicit, so a wrong pick can only mean "the wrong one of *your own*
  clinics" — never another clinic's data.

The practical consequence: through the current client, an account cannot end up in two clinics at all,
so `limit=1` is unambiguous. It becomes a real problem only if someone is added to a second clinic
directly through SQL or the dashboard — which is why the patch in §7 should be applied before
multi-clinic use.

