## 4. `SECURITY DEFINER` functions — privileges, ownership, search path

| Function | Definer? | `search_path` pinned | EXECUTE granted to |
|---|---|---|---|
| `my_clinics`, `my_owned_clinics`, `my_role_in`, `is_member_of` | yes | `public, pg_temp` | `authenticated` |
| `can_read`, `can_write_clinical`, `can_write_decisions`, `can_manage` | yes | `public, pg_temp` | `authenticated` |
| `safe_uuid` | no (plain immutable) | n/a | `authenticated` |
| `my_memberships`, `patient_read`, `log_access` | yes | `public, pg_temp` | `authenticated` |
| `bootstrap_clinic`, `add_clinic_member` (both forms), `remove_clinic_member` | yes | `public, pg_temp` | `authenticated` |
| `audit_trigger`, `touch_updated_at` | definer / plain | `public, pg_temp` | see note |

**Default privileges revoked.** Every function above is revoked from `PUBLIC` and from `anon`, then
granted only to `authenticated`. Without this, PostgreSQL's default `EXECUTE TO PUBLIC` would let an
anonymous request call `patient_read` — a `SECURITY DEFINER` function that bypasses RLS. That was a
genuine hole in revision 1 and is the single most important change in this file.

`revoke all on schema public from anon` is also applied.

**Trigger functions — a deliberate, flagged choice.** `audit_trigger` and `touch_updated_at` keep
`EXECUTE` for `authenticated`. PostgreSQL does not re-check `EXECUTE` when a trigger fires, and a
function returning `trigger` cannot be called directly, so withholding it would add no safety — but
since I cannot execute this script, I left it rather than change it blind. After verifying that writes
still audit correctly you can tighten it:

```sql
revoke all on function public.audit_trigger() from authenticated;
revoke all on function public.touch_updated_at() from authenticated;
```

**Ownership.** All objects should be owned by `postgres` (the role the SQL editor runs as). If any
function is owned by an application role, that role can redefine it. Verify with:

```sql
select p.proname, pg_get_userbyid(p.proowner) as owner
from pg_proc p join pg_namespace n on n.oid = p.pronamespace
where n.nspname = 'public' and p.proname in ('patient_read','my_clinics','can_read');
```

Expected owner: `postgres`.

