## 5. RLS coverage

| Object | RLS | Policies |
|---|---|---|
| `clinics` | enabled | select (members) |
| `clinic_members` | enabled | select (self or members), update (owner of that clinic). **No insert, no delete** — denied outright; membership changes only via RPC |
| `patients`, `measurements`, `labs`, `assessments`, `plans`, `followups`, `attachments`, `recipes`, `kb` | enabled | 4 each (read/insert/update/delete), role-aware |
| `audit_log` | enabled | select only. **No insert policy** — entries come from the trigger, so a client cannot forge one |
| `storage.objects` (bucket `clinical-documents`) | bucket is private | read (any member), insert/update (can_write_clinical), delete (can_write_decisions) |

**Clinic A cannot reach clinic B**, by four separate mechanisms: the `using` clause on every read, the
`with check` on every write, the storage path prefix check, and the `SECURITY DEFINER` helpers being
clinic-explicit. `patient_read` returns `null` for a foreign patient — indistinguishable from "not
found", so it does not confirm the existence of another clinic's record either.

**Row reassignment is blocked.** No policy narrows the columns an UPDATE may change, so a client could
try to rewrite `clinic_id`. The `with check` clause re-evaluates the capability for the *new* value,
and the caller has no authority in the target clinic, so the update is refused.

**What is *not* protected, by design:** `service_role` and the table owner bypass RLS. This schema
does not use `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`, because forcing it would also restrict dashboard
administration. Anyone holding the `service_role` key reads and writes everything. That key must never
leave the server.

---

## 6. Read paths — which reads are audited, and which are not

PostgreSQL has **no SELECT trigger**, so a read cannot be audited by the database. Auditing requires
wrapping the read in a function. Starting from that constraint, here is the complete inventory:

| # | Read path | Location | Audited? |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Opening a patient record (`patient_read` RPC) | `app.js` → `setPatient` | **YES — server-guaranteed** |
| 2 | Restoring the last patient on page load | `app.js` → `restorePatient` | **No** |
| 3 | Patient picker list | `app.js` → `openPatientPicker` | **No** |
| 4 | Dashboard patient list and counts | `views-core.js` (Dashboard) | **No** |
| 5 | Patients module list and search | `views-core.js` (Patients) | **No** |
| 6 | Patient edit form load | `views-core.js` → `openEditor` | **No** |
| 7 | Next-patient-code computation | `views-core.js` (New patient save) | **No** |
| 8 | All clinical reads by patient — measurements, labs, assessments, plans, follow-ups | `store.byPatient(...)` across the clinical, planning and library views | **No** |

**So: exactly one read path is guaranteed, and eight are not.** Path 1 covers the moment a clinician
opens a record, which is the event an inspection most often asks about. Paths 3–8 are lists and
lookups; auditing each would flood the log with thousands of aggregate reads.

**To close the gap** you would route *all* clinical reads through server functions — one per entity, or
a single `clinic_read(entity, filters)` — so no data can be fetched outside an audited path. That is a
deliberate architectural step with a real cost (an RPC per list view). If non-repudiable read auditing
is an inspection requirement, it is the correct answer, and it is worth doing before go-live rather
than retrofitting.

**Already non-repudiable:** every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE on the nine clinical tables is logged by
database trigger. The audit table has no INSERT policy, so clients cannot forge or amend entries.

