## 3. Security review of the backend (directive §4)

I reviewed my own schema and **found and fixed a real privilege-escalation flaw**.

### 3.1 Flaw found and fixed

The membership policy read:

```sql
using (public.my_role() = 'owner')
```

and `my_role()` was `select role … where user_id = auth.uid() limit 1` — **no row scoping, no
ordering**. For a user belonging to two clinics the returned role was nondeterministic, and the policy
never referenced the row it was guarding. An owner of clinic B who was also a member of clinic A could
therefore modify membership rows in clinic A — including promoting themselves.

Fixed by resolving ownership as a **set of clinics** and scoping the policy to the row:

```sql
using (clinic_id in (select public.my_owned_clinics()))
with check (clinic_id in (select public.my_owned_clinics()))
```

`my_role()` was deleted outright, with a comment explaining why a scalar role helper must not return.

### 3.2 Other hardening in the same pass

| Issue | Fix |
|---|---|
| Storage policies cast a path segment with `::uuid`. An unparseable name made the whole policy evaluation **raise** rather than deny. | Added `safe_uuid()`, which returns null instead of raising. |
| `log_access` accepted any patient id, so a client could write fabricated entries into the audit trail. | It now resolves the clinic **from the patient row**, restricted to the caller's memberships, and silently ignores anything else. |
| `log_access` also used the same unscoped `limit 1` clinic lookup. | Removed; the clinic now comes from the patient. |

### 3.3 How to test isolation yourself (after standing up the backend)

Run these in the Supabase SQL editor, substituting two real account ids.

**Test A — cross-clinic isolation.** As user 1 (clinic A), with a patient belonging to clinic B:

```sql
select * from public.patients where id = '<clinic-B-patient-id>';
```

**Expected: zero rows.** Not an error — simply nothing, because the RLS policy filters it out.

**Test B — the audited read refuses a foreign patient.**

```sql
select public.patient_read('<clinic-B-patient-id>');
```

**Expected: `null`.** And **no audit_log row** should be created. Confirm with:

```sql
select * from public.audit_log order by at desc limit 5;
```

**Test C — membership cannot be self-modified.** As a `clinician` (not an owner):

```sql
update public.clinic_members set role = 'owner' where user_id = auth.uid();
```

**Expected: 0 rows updated.** No error necessarily, but nothing changes. Verify by re-reading the row.

**Test D — membership cannot be inserted directly.**

```sql
insert into public.clinic_members (clinic_id, user_id, role)
values ('<any-clinic-id>', auth.uid(), 'owner');
```

**Expected: an RLS policy violation.** There is no INSERT policy, so this is denied outright.

**Test E — onboarding refuses a second clinic.** Call `bootstrap_clinic` twice:

```sql
select public.bootstrap_clinic('Second Clinic');
```

**Expected the second call to raise:** *“This account already belongs to a clinic”*.

**Test F — documents are private.** In an incognito window, paste a `clinical-documents` object URL
directly.

**Expected: denied.** The bucket is private; only a signed URL issued to a clinic member works.

### 3.4 Residual risks I am not claiming to have solved

| Risk | Status |
|---|---|
| An owner can enumerate whether an email has an account, via `add_clinic_member` | **Accepted.** Only owners can call it. Documented rather than hidden. |
| `bootstrap_clinic` could race with itself and create two clinics for one account | **Accepted, low.** Requires two simultaneous first-time requests. A unique index on a derived key would close it. |
| **Destructive actions are not role-gated server-side.** Any clinic member can delete a patient or wipe records through the REST API. | **Open.** The interface gates these by role, but a determined client can bypass the interface. Closing it properly means moving writes behind RPCs with role checks — see §5. |
| Settings are per-device, not shared | **By design.** Documented in the README. |

