## 4. Read auditing — what is guaranteed and what is not (directive §5)

**PostgreSQL has no SELECT trigger.** A read cannot be audited by the database. Anything that audits
reads must therefore wrap the read in a function. That constraint drives everything below.

| Read path | Mechanism | Guarantee |
|---|---|---|
| Opening a patient record | `patient_read(id)` — a `security definer` function that reads and writes the audit row in **one server-side transaction** | **Guaranteed.** If the row comes back, the entry exists. A client cannot obtain the record without the entry. |
| Everything else — patient lists, measurements, plans, labs, recipes, knowledge base | Not separately audited | **Not guaranteed.** These are ordinary REST selects. |
| If the audited call fails | The app falls back to a plain read **and tells the user** with a warning toast | Visible, but the read is then unaudited. Recorded in the console. |

**To close the remaining gap** you would route *all* clinical reads through server functions — one per
entity, or a single `clinic_read(entity, filters)` — so that no data can be fetched outside an audited
path. That is a deliberate architectural step with a real performance cost (an RPC per list view), and
it is the correct answer if non-repudiable read auditing is an inspection requirement. The schema
comments say so as well, so this does not get lost.

**What is already non-repudiable:** every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE on clinical tables is logged by
database trigger. A client cannot skip it, and the log is read-only to clients.

