## 5. Verifying shared persistence across two devices (directive §6)

Requires the backend from §6 of the README to be configured first. **Use synthetic data only.**

| Step | Action | Expected |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | On device A, sign in and register a patient named `Test Patient AA`, date of birth 1990-01-01 | Saved without error; the header pill shows the name |
| 2 | On device A, record a measurement — weight 80 kg | Visible in Body Composition |
| 3 | Reload device A | The patient and the measurement are still there |
| 4 | On device B (another browser, another machine, or a private window), sign in as the **same** account | The Dashboard appears |
| 5 | Device B opens Patients | **`Test Patient AA` is listed** — this is the proof of shared persistence |
| 6 | Device B opens the record | Weight 80 kg is present, and **the audited read creates an audit row** |
| 7 | Device B changes the weight to 79 kg | Saved |
| 8 | Device A reloads and reopens the record | Shows **79 kg** — changes propagate |
| 9 | Check the audit log | Entries for both reads and for the update, with different actors/timestamps |
| 10 | Sign in as a **different** account in a different clinic | **`Test Patient AA` must not appear anywhere.** If it does, stop: isolation is broken. |
| 11 | Delete `Test Patient AA` when finished | Removed on both devices |

**If step 5 fails** (the record does not appear on device B), the likely causes in order: the anon key
or URL is wrong on one device; the second device is not signed in; or the schema was not run, so RLS
has no policy and every query returns nothing.

