## 3.5 Third-pass review — completion pass

A further pass completed the outstanding brief requirements and re-examined the areas where a static
claim would have been worthless.

### Requirements completed

| Brief | Delivered |
|---|---|
| §12 | **Recipes integrated into the Plan Builder.** Recipes are presented through the same food shape as the food table, so plan generation, exchange, recalculation and reporting need no special case. A recipe defaults to one serving; its nutrient values are per 100 g of the finished dish, derived from the ingredient list and the yield. |
| §12 | **Smart Exchange covers recipes.** A recipe can be swapped out for a food, a food for a recipe, and recipes compete on the same ranking. |
| §11 | **Knowledge base.** Text, Markdown, CSV, TSV and JSON are parsed, chunked and searchable, and feed the Copilot's retrieval. PDF, Word and Excel are recognised and *refused honestly* — stored for reference with an explicit statement that their text was not read. A documented remote adapter closes the gap when a server exists. |
| §9 | **Copilot context completed.** The copilot now also receives follow-up history and the recorded adherence, and the de-identified outbound payload carries approved targets and follow-up state — with an assertion that a patient name never survives into it. |
| §4 | **Blood pressure** recorded into the append-only measurement history and printed on reports. |
| §5 | **Deficit bands renamed to Mild / Moderate / High** as the brief specifies, and the Energy screen now shows the whole chain — BMR → TDEE → deficit/surplus → approved target → diet plan — with each stage's completion state. |
| §17 | **The report template is editable**: accent colour and a type scale (small / normal / large), applied through CSS custom properties and an em-based print stylesheet, alongside the existing branded/plain modes, heading, and section toggles. Arabic and English both honoured. |
| §19 | **The client-side API key field was removed.** It was a security anti-pattern: any key entered into a browser-only build is readable by anyone who opens the page. The adapter now calls a proxy and sends no credential. |

### Defects found and fixed in this pass

| # | Defect | Impact had it shipped |
|---|---|---|
| 13 | `planner.addItem` resolved foods with `CN.foods.byId` only | **Recipes could never be added to a plan** — the headline requirement of this pass would have failed silently, returning `null` |
| 14 | A recipe's allergens were taken from its own list, which is normally empty | **A clinical safety hole.** A composite dish containing peanut would have looked allergen-free, so a peanut-allergic patient could be served it. Allergens are now inherited from the ingredients — which is precisely the "hidden ingredient" case brief §8 calls out. |
| 15 | `restrictionViolations` skipped unresolvable ids | Recipe items bypassed the plan's restriction warning entirely |
| 16 | Duplicate `var food` declaration in the report writer | Confusing, and a trap for the next edit |
| 17 | Three display paths still used `byId` | A recipe item would have shown a bare `recipe:rcp_...` id on screen, in the exchange drawer, and in the version diff |
| 18 | One i18n key was accidentally dropped during editing (`rp.saved`) | The Reports screen would have shown "rp.saved" as its toast |

Defect 14 is the most serious of this pass: it was a latent patient-safety flaw, not a cosmetic one.

### On binary integrity — the specific concern raised

A previous pass fixed blob handling in the in-memory clone and claimed the corruption risk was closed.
That claim was too strong, and this pass addresses it properly rather than restating it:

- **The backup no longer drops binaries.** It encodes attachment contents as base64 and reports the
  count and byte total in the envelope. `restore()` decodes them back to `Blob` objects.
- **The claim is now testable, and tested by construction.** `tests/engine-tests.html` contains a
  storage suite that writes a real attachment with multi-byte content, takes a real backup, verifies
  the bytes **decode correctly straight out of the backup document**, restores, and asserts the
  restored bytes match the original exactly. It also covers a version-1 backup upgrading in place.
- **That suite has still not been executed.** It is written to be self-verifying and its probe records
  are cleaned up afterwards. Until it is run, the correct statement remains: binary round-tripping is
  *implemented and covered by an unexecuted test*, not *verified*.

The suite deliberately avoids wiping storage, so running it cannot destroy real records.

