## 3.6 Fourth-pass review — audit and server backend

### Defect 19 — CRITICAL: every write in the application was lost

While refactoring the storage facade to add the backend, I read this line in `store.js` and stopped:

```js
put(s, obj) {
  if (!obj.id) obj.id = U.uid(s.slice(0, 3));
  return backend.put(obj);        // ← one argument
}
```

Every adapter implements `put(storeName, object)`. Calling it with one argument passed the **record**
as the store name and left the row `undefined`. The consequences differed by tier, and both were bad:

| Tier | Effect |
|---|---|
| localStorage (what `file://` uses) | `read(obj)` looks up `"cn.store.[object Object]"`, finds nothing, then writes `[null]`. **The write resolves without error and the record is gone.** |
| in-memory | Same silent path. Returns `undefined` having stored nothing. |
| IndexedDB | `db.transaction(storeName)` receives an object, throws, and the save rejects. |

**No version of this application could ever have persisted a patient, a measurement, a plan, a recipe,
an attachment, a knowledge-base document or a setting.** On the file:// path — the one shipped as the
zero-setup option — the failure was silent: the interface would have looked as though it saved, and
the record would not exist. That is the worst failure mode a clinical tool can have, and it is a
direct vindication of the insistence on running the tests rather than trusting a review.

Fixed by forwarding the store: `backendFor(s).put(s, obj)`. A dedicated regression test now asserts
that a record written through `put()` can be read back and that no row is written as `undefined`.

### Defects 20 and 21 — the initial screen

| # | Defect | Impact |
|---|---|---|
| 20 | `firstRunCheck` opened a **non-dismissable** modal on first launch | A new user — or anyone opening the link on a phone — met a clinic-identity form before ever seeing the application. Removed entirely; the Dashboard now shows a dismissible card instead. |
| 21 | With zero patients the Dashboard rendered **only** an empty state and returned early | Every other panel — stats, reporting, and all navigation — was unreachable until a patient had been registered. The hub is now rendered first, unconditionally. |

### Server backend implemented

| Deliverable | Where |
|---|---|
| Auth, REST data layer, private object storage, remote adapter | `site/js/backend.js` |
| Tables, RLS policies, audit triggers, storage policies, onboarding RPCs | `docs/supabase-schema.sql` |
| Settings UI — connect, test, sign in, sign up, sign out, live status | `site/js/views-core.js` |
| Configuration template (no values, anon key only) | `site/config.example.js` |
| Remote document download via short-lived signed URLs | `site/js/views-clinical.js` |
| Read-access logging when a patient record is opened | `site/js/app.js` |

### Tests added this pass

Regression for the `put()` defect; settings save/reload; backend defaults and refusal of an
unconfigured connection; capability reporting; Dashboard registration. The suite now also loads
`views-core.js`, so a load-order fault there surfaces as a named failure rather than silence.

### Honest status of the backend

**Implemented and unverified.** The adapter, authentication flow, signed-URL path, schema, RLS
policies and audit triggers are written. What has never happened is a live round-trip: that needs a
Supabase project, which requires the user's account. Standing up a project and running the schema is
the remaining action, and it is theirs.

