# NutriClinic Pro — Clinical Nutrition & Patient Management Platform

A premium clinical nutrition workstation for a private clinic: electronic patient records, deterministic
energy calculations, therapeutic diet planning, smart food exchange, a patient-aware copilot, follow-up
management and A4 report output. Bilingual English / Arabic with full RTL support.

**Static, self-contained build.** No build step, no dependencies, no CDN, no external fonts — every
asset ships in-site, satisfying a strict CSP.

---

## ⚠️ Scope and limitations — read before using clinically

This application was **authored from scratch against the original 23-section brief**. It is honest
about what it does and does not do.

| | Status |
|---|---|
| All 13 modules, deterministic engine, persistence, plan generation, smart exchange, PDF output | **Delivered** |
| Bilingual EN/AR with RTL | **Delivered** |
| Local document attachments — lab and body-composition files (brief §4) | **Delivered** — held in the browser, downloadable back; excluded from backups |
| Blood pressure recording (brief §4) | **Delivered** — stored in the same append-only measurement history |
| Clinician-defined custom fields (brief §3) | **Delivered** — defined in Settings, no source change |
| Clinic reporting with search and filters (brief §18) | **Delivered** |
| Custom recipes — nutrition computed from ingredients and finished yield, selectable in the Plan Builder and in Smart Exchange (brief §12) | **Delivered** |
| Knowledge base — text, Markdown, CSV, TSV and JSON ingested, chunked and searchable (brief §11) | **Delivered** |
| Knowledge base — PDF, Word and Excel text extraction | **Not implemented** — the format is recognised, the file is kept for reference, and the interface states plainly that its text was not read. Needs a server-side indexer. |
| PostgreSQL, server API, authentication, RBAC, encryption at rest, audit log, backups (brief §§19, 21) | **Not implemented** |
| Live internet research with source attribution (brief §12) | **Not implemented** — and stated as such in the UI |
| LLM-backed Copilot (brief §9) | **Not implemented** — the shipped copilot is a rule-based interpreter that says so. A proxy adapter is wired and documented. |

**Clinical content is a synthesis, not a transcription.** The food table, the 20 approaches and the
clinical briefs were written from established nutrition science and standard reference values. They are
internally consistent and clinically sane, but they are **not** derived from the uploaded lecture PDFs —
those could not be read in the authoring environment. All of it lives in three `data-*.js` files, so
replacing it with your own material is mechanical.

**Do not put real patient data in it.** Storage is browser-local with no authentication and no audit
trail. See `docs/backend-and-security.md` for what closing that gap requires.

---

## Quick start

Three ways to run it, from simplest to most capable.

### 1. Just open it (no tools needed)

Double-click **`site/index.html`**. Everything works — dashboard, patients, calculations,
plans, recipes, copilot, reports. Records persist across reloads in browser storage.

*Limit:* browser storage is capped at a few megabytes here, so keep document attachments small.
If it fills up, the app tells you instead of losing the record silently.

### 2. Local server (recommended on this machine)

Double-click **`Start-NutriClinic.bat`** in the folder above `site/`. It starts a server, opens the
app, and uses IndexedDB — so records and documents are stored in full.

If Python is missing, the script says so and opens the app anyway.

*Port clash:* use `Start-NutriClinic.ps1`, which picks a free port automatically.

### 3. Network mode — phone, tablet, another computer

Double-click **`Start-NutriClinic-Mobile.bat`**. It serves the app to your whole network and prints
the exact `http://…` address to type on the other device.

```
http://192.168.1.x:8617/      ← the script prints your real address
```

Requirements and cautions:

- The other device must be on the **same network**.
- Windows will ask whether to let Python through the firewall — choose **Private networks** and Allow,
  or other devices cannot connect.
- **There is no login yet.** Anyone on that network can open the app. Use it on a network you trust,
  and stop the server (Ctrl+C) when you are done.
- **Each device keeps its own records.** Data lives in that device's browser storage, so a patient
  registered on your phone does **not** appear on your desktop. Only the server build (below) gives
  one shared record set.

### 4. A public link other devices can reach over the internet

This project cannot deploy itself — there is no deployment tool here, and I will not invent a URL.
But `site/` is a plain static folder with relative paths and no build step, so it can be dropped onto
any static host unchanged:

| Host | Steps |
|---|---|
| **Netlify Drop** | Go to `app.netlify.com/drop`, drag the **`site`** folder onto the page. You get an HTTPS link in seconds. |
| **Cloudflare Pages** | Create a project, choose "direct upload", upload the contents of `site/`. |
| **GitHub Pages** | Commit the contents of `site/` to a repo and enable Pages. |

`site/_headers` adds security headers on hosts that honour it (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages).

**Read this before hosting publicly:** a public link serves the *application*, not your data. Each
visitor's records live in their own browser, so nobody sees yours — and your devices still do not
share records with each other. A public URL makes the app reachable; it does not make the data
shared or secure. For that you need the server build described in `docs/`.

---

## Server backend — shared records, login and audit (optional)

The application runs fully without a server. Connecting one adds what a browser alone cannot
provide: **one shared record set across authorised devices, authentication, role-based access and a
server-side audit trail.**

It uses a plain Supabase project (PostgreSQL + Auth + private Storage). No SDK is loaded — the app
talks to Supabase's REST, Auth and Storage HTTP APIs with `fetch`, so the no-external-assets
constraint still holds.

### Exact steps — about ten minutes, and they need your account

I cannot create this for you: it requires your Supabase login. Everything on the application side is
already implemented and merged; only the project has to exist.

1. **Create the project.** `supabase.com` → New project. Choose a region close to the clinic and
   save the database password somewhere safe.
2. **Create the schema.** Open the SQL editor, paste the whole of `docs/supabase-schema.sql`, run it.
   It creates the tables, enables row-level security, adds the audit triggers, creates the private
   `clinical-documents` bucket and installs the onboarding functions.
3. **Decide on email confirmation.** Authentication → Providers → Email. With confirmation ON, a new
   user must click a link before signing in. Turning it OFF is faster for a single-clinic pilot but
   means unverified addresses can register.
4. **Copy two values.** Project Settings → API: the **Project URL** and the **anon / public** key.
5. **Put them in the app.** Either
   - copy `site/config.example.js` to `site/config.js` and fill in those two values (recommended for
     a deployment), or
   - open **Settings → Server backend** in the running app and paste them there (stored on that
     device only).
6. **Create the first account and clinic.** Settings → Server backend → enter an email and a secret →
   **Create account and clinic**. The first account becomes the clinic **owner**.
7. **Add colleagues.** Have each person register, then on the owner's device call the
   `add_clinic_member` function with their email and a role (`clinician`, `assistant` or `auditor`).
   Only an owner can add members, which keeps privilege escalation out of the clinical role.
8. **Verify.** Press **Test connection** — it performs a real request and reports the failure reason
   if anything is wrong, rather than showing a hopeful tick.

### What the security model actually guarantees

| Concern | How it is handled |
|---|---|
| Key exposure | Only the **anon** key ships. It is public by design and grants nothing without a signed-in clinic member. The **service_role** key bypasses every rule — it is never in this codebase, and must never be put in `config.js`. |
| Cross-clinic access | Every clinical table has RLS enabled and one policy: `clinic_id in (select my_clinics())`. A signed-in user sees only their own clinic's rows. |
| Self-promotion | Membership is writable only through `add_clinic_member`, which refuses any caller who is not an owner. |
| Documents | The storage bucket is **private**. Objects sit under `<clinic_id>/…` and are reached only via short-lived signed URLs. |
| Modification audit | Written by **trigger**, so a client cannot skip it. The clinical payload is deliberately not copied into the log — the entry records who changed which record when, without duplicating patient data into a second table. |
| Read audit | Recorded through an explicit RPC when a patient record is opened. **This is client-reported** and therefore weaker: see the honesty note below. |

**Read audit is not tamper-proof.** A client that never calls `log_access` leaves no read record. Write
audit cannot be bypassed; read audit can. If non-repudiable read auditing is a requirement, move data
access behind a server function instead of the REST client used here. The schema file says this too —
I would rather you know the limit than discover it in an inspection.

### Honest status

**Implemented in the application; not yet exercised against a live project.** The adapter, the
authentication flow, the signed-URL document path, the schema, the RLS policies and the audit
triggers are all written. What has never happened is a real round-trip, because that needs a Supabase
